  "references": [
    "K811v2: VVIX conditioning \u2014 VIX r=-0.327 > VVIX r=-0.148 for MDD",
    "K41: VT = ~4%/yr constant insurance",
    "K229: VT Insurance Pricing \u2014 3.05%/yr expected cost",
    "K687: No VT beats BH 50/50 on Sharpe after correct lag",
    "K688: VT wins under CRRA utility gamma >= 5",
    "Harvey, Liu, Zhu (2016) t>3.0 threshold"
  ],
  "conclusions": {
    "summary": "VIX-percentile conditioning provides negligible improvement over always-VT (S1). S2 Sharpe 0.4538 vs S1 0.4525 — statistically indistinguishable. S3 (smooth scaling) is significantly WORSE. Neither S2 nor S3 beats BH SPY or 50/50 on Sharpe.",
    "key_findings": [
      "S2 VIX-Pctile Cond reduces insurance cost by only 0.5% vs S1 — economically negligible",
      "S3 VIX-Smooth INCREASES insurance cost by 37.5% vs S1 — over-scaling down",
      "VIX percentile monotonically predicts forward 30d MDD (Q1=-3.1% to Q5=-7.9%)",
      "Cross-OOS: S2 beats BH only 1/5, S3 beats BH only 1/5 — both fail Cross-OOS",
      "No VT strategy beats 50/50 SPY/GLD in any OOS period (0/5)",
      "DM test: S3 vs S0 t=3.11 (significant), but S3 has LOWER Sharpe — significance is for volatility reduction, not better Sharpe",
      "Sensitivity: Sharpe varies only 1% across threshold range 10-90 to 30-70 — very robust but also means thresholds don't matter",
      "K811v2 VVIX conditioning achieved lower insurance cost (2.2% vs 4.2%) but used different period — not directly comparable"
    ],
    "implication": "VIX percentile is a good MDD predictor but conditioning on it does NOT materially reduce VT insurance cost. The problem is that VIX is already embedded in 12/VIX — high VIX automatically reduces equity weight. Adding a percentile overlay on top is redundant. This confirms VIX sufficiency from yet another angle."
  }
}